//? Title: Backtracking Search: Simple Paths and Knight's Tours
//? Subtitle: How large the tree of simple paths is, why exhausting it is hopeless, and how branch ordering and pruning rescue the search for Hamiltonian paths, with datamunge's algorithms::count_simple_paths and algorithms::hamiltonian_path
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Backtracking extends a partial solution one choice at a time and undoes the last choice when no
extension works. On a graph, the partial solutions are simple paths, and the search tree is the
tree of all simple paths from the start. We derive its size for complete graphs, where the
number of simple paths between two vertices is exactly \(\lfloor e\,(n-2)!\rfloor\), and count it
by exhaustion on grids, where the corner-to-corner paths grow from 12 on a \(3\times3\) grid to
1,262,816 on a \(6\times6\) grid. Looking for one path that visits every vertex (a Hamiltonian
path) explores the same tree, so the order of branches and the ability to cut dead subtrees
decide everything. On the knight's graph of a chessboard, trying moves in index order solves
only 6 of the 64 starting squares within a million nodes; a connectivity prune raises that
to 30; Warnsdorff's rule, which tries the most constrained square first, solves all 64
with a median of 64 nodes, meaning no backtracking at all. The same rule still stalls on some
larger boards, which is where backtracking earns its keep.
)

\toc

> The algorithm

Backtracking keeps a *simple* path \(v_0v_1\cdots v_k\) (no repeated vertex) and a mark on each
vertex currently on it:

```
SEARCH(u):
    append u to the path and mark it
    if u is a goal: report the path (stop, or continue for all solutions)
    for each unmarked neighbour v of u: SEARCH(v)
    unmark u and remove it from the path        # backtrack
```

The marks are the invariant: a vertex is marked exactly while it is on the path, so the path is
always simple, and every reported path is valid. Every simple path from the start corresponds to
exactly one call of `SEARCH`, so the calls form the *tree of simple paths*: the root is the start,
and each node's children are its one-vertex extensions. Memory is only the path and the marks,
\(O(V)\), but time is the size of the tree, which is usually exponential.

`dm::algorithms::backtracking_search()` stops at the first path it finds. On a \(4\times4\) grid
from one corner to the other it follows the lowest-numbered neighbour each time:

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto edges = alg::grid_graph_edges(4, 4);  // vertex (r, c) is 4r + c
  const auto first = alg::backtracking_search(16, edges, 0, 15, false);
  std::print("First path found: ");
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < first.path.size(); ++k) std::print("{}{}", k ? " → " : "", first.path[k]);
  std::print(" ({} edges; the shortest has 6). Calls before reaching the goal: {}.\n\n", first.path.size() - 1,
             first.order.size());
  const auto all = alg::count_simple_paths(16, edges, 0, 15);
  std::print("Exhausting the tree instead: {} simple paths from 0 to 15, {} search nodes, {} dead ends, longest path {} edges.\n",
             all.paths, all.nodes, all.dead_ends, all.max_depth);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
First path found: 0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 7 → 6 → 5 → 4 → 8 → 9 → 10 → 11 → 15 (12 edges; the shortest has 6). Calls before reaching the goal: 13.

Exhausting the tree instead: 184 simple paths from 0 to 15, 1271 search nodes, 208 dead ends, longest path 14 edges.
// result_end

The first path found is not short: always taking the lowest-numbered neighbour sweeps the grid
row by row and reaches the goal after 12 edges, twice the 6 of a shortest path. Backtracking
answers "is there a path?", not "which is best?"; for shortest paths, breadth-first search or
Dijkstra's algorithm visit each vertex once. Here the first branch happened to succeed, so 13
calls sufficed. Listing *every* path is a different matter: the \(4\times4\) grid has 184 simple
corner-to-corner paths, and finding them took 1,271 search nodes, 208 of them dead ends where the
path had trapped itself.

> How big is the tree?

>> Complete graphs, exactly

In the complete graph \(K_n\) every ordering of distinct vertices is a path. A simple path from
\(s\) to \(t\) with \(k\) intermediate vertices chooses them, in order, from the other \(n-2\):
\((n-2)!/(n-2-k)!\) ways. Summing over \(k\) and substituting \(j=n-2-k\),

$$
P_n=\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}\frac{(n-2)!}{(n-2-k)!}=(n-2)!\sum_{j=0}^{n-2}\frac1{j!}=\bigl\lfloor e\,(n-2)!\bigr\rfloor\quad(n\ge3).
$$
\alttext(P n equals the sum over k from 0 to n minus 2 of n minus 2 factorial over n minus 2 minus k factorial, which equals n minus 2 factorial times the sum over j of 1 over j factorial, which equals the floor of e times n minus 2 factorial, for n at least 3.)

The last step holds because the tail \((n-2)!\sum_{j>n-2}1/j!\) is positive and, for \(n\ge4\),
below \(\tfrac1{n-1}+\tfrac1{(n-1)^2}+\cdots=\tfrac1{n-2}<1\) (and equals \(e-2<1\) at \(n=3\)).
The search tree has one node for every path from \(s\) that avoids \(t\) except perhaps at its
end. Those ending at \(t\) number \(P_n\); those ending elsewhere are the same choices without the
final step to \(t\), also \(P_n\). So the tree has exactly \(2P_n\) nodes.

>> Grids, by exhaustion

On a grid there is no closed form. The number of corner-to-corner simple paths on a \(k\times k\)
grid of vertices is sequence A007764 in the OEIS, and it is known that it grows like
\(\lambda^{k^2}\) with \(\lambda\approx1.7445\) per vertex. The block counts both families with
`count_simple_paths()`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backtracking_search_growth.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| \\(n\\) | paths in \\(K_n\\) | \\(\\lfloor e(n-2)!\\rfloor\\) | search nodes | \\(2P_n\\) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> kn_v, kn_nodes;
  for (std::size_t n = 3; n <= 11; ++n) {
    const auto c = alg::count_simple_paths(n, alg::complete_graph_edges(n), 0, n - 1);
    double f = 1;
    for (std::size_t k = 2; k <= n - 2; ++k) f *= static_cast<double>(k);
    const auto formula = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(std::floor(std::numbers::e * f));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, c.paths, formula, c.nodes, 2 * formula);
    kn_v.push_back(static_cast<double>(n));
    kn_nodes.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(c.nodes)));
  }

  std::print("\n| grid | vertices | corner-to-corner paths | search nodes | dead ends | nodes per path | "
             "\\(\\text{{paths}}^{{1/k^2}}\\) | ms |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> grid_v, grid_nodes;
  for (std::size_t k = 2; k <= 6; ++k) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    const auto c = alg::count_simple_paths(k * k, alg::grid_graph_edges(k, k), 0, k * k - 1);
    const double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    std::print("| {}×{} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {:.1f} |\n", k, k, k * k, c.paths, c.nodes, c.dead_ends,
               static_cast<double>(c.nodes) / c.paths, std::pow(static_cast<double>(c.paths), 1.0 / (k * k)), ms);
    grid_v.push_back(static_cast<double>(k * k));
    grid_nodes.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(c.nodes)));
  }

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(kn_v, kn_nodes, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .points(kn_v, kn_nodes, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 7.0)
      .lines(grid_v, grid_nodes, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(grid_v, grid_nodes, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 7.0)
      .legend({"complete graph K_n", "k x k grid"}, {dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}})
      .y_limits(0, 9)
      .title("Size of the backtracking tree")
      .x_label("number of vertices")
      .y_label("log10 search nodes")
      .size(900, 500)
      .save_svg("build/backtracking_search_growth.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(n\) | paths in \(K_n\) | \(\lfloor e(n-2)!\rfloor\) | search nodes | \(2P_n\) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
