| 6×6 | 36 | 1262816 | 18470411 | 4008910 | 14.6 | 1.477 | 102.9 |
\image(figures/backtracking_search_growth.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Search-tree size, on a log scale, against the number of vertices: complete graphs \(K_3\) to \(K_{11}\) (red) and \(k\times k\) grids for \(k=2\) to 6 (blue).)
\alttext(Line chart of log10 search nodes against the number of vertices. The red complete-graph line rises steeply and ever faster, from 0.6 at 3 vertices to 6.3 at 11. The blue grid line rises nearly straight, from 0.7 at 4 vertices to 7.3 at 36.)

For the complete graphs the counts equal \(\lfloor e(n-2)!\rfloor\) and the node counts equal
\(2P_n\) for every \(n\) from 3 to 11, as derived; at \(n=11\) there are already 986,410 paths
and nearly two million nodes. The red curve bends upwards because factorial growth is faster
than any exponential.

The grids grow more slowly but still exponentially: the blue curve is close to a straight line
in the number of vertices. The per-vertex growth \(\text{paths}^{1/k^2}\) rises from 1.19 to 1.48
and is still far from its limit of about 1.74, so the next grid, \(7\times7\), has 575,780,564
paths and would need roughly \(10^{10}\) nodes. The 6×6 grid took 18.5 million nodes, about 90 ms
on this machine. The ratio of nodes to paths grows too, from 2.5 to 14.6, and over a fifth of the
nodes are dead ends. Exhaustive backtracking is only useful for small instances.

> Hamiltonian paths: order and pruning

A *Hamiltonian path* visits every vertex once. Deciding whether one exists is NP-complete, and
backtracking searches the same tree of simple paths, stopping at the first path of length
\(V-1\). Two levers change how much of the tree it sees:

- **Branch order.** Trying the most constrained neighbour first, the one with the fewest
  unvisited neighbours of its own, tends to visit awkward vertices before they are cut off.
  For knight's tours this is *Warnsdorff's rule* (1823).
- **Pruning.** If the unvisited vertices are not all reachable from the end of the path through
  unvisited vertices, no completion exists, and the whole subtree can be skipped. The test is one
  graph traversal, \(O(V+E)\), per node.

The knight's graph joins two squares of a board when a knight can move between them. A
Hamiltonian path in it is an open *knight's tour*. A knight always changes square colour, so a
tour alternates colours. On a board with an odd number of squares the tour must start and end on
the majority colour; from any other square, no tour exists and backtracking must exhaust the tree
to prove it.

The block runs `hamiltonian_path()` from every square of the \(8\times8\) board in three ways,
with a limit of a million nodes per search, and draws the tour from the corner.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backtracking_search_tour.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::size_t k = 8, n = k * k;
  const auto edges = alg::knight_graph_edges(k, k);
  struct Method {
    const char* name;
    alg::BranchOrder order;
    bool prune;
  };
  const Method methods[] = {{"index order", alg::BranchOrder::Index, false},
                            {"index order + connectivity prune", alg::BranchOrder::Index, true},
                            {"Warnsdorff order", alg::BranchOrder::FewestOnward, false}};
  std::print("| method | tours found (of 64) | stopped at limit | median nodes | largest nodes (solved) | total s |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& m : methods) {
    std::vector<std::uint64_t> nodes;
    std::uint64_t worst_solved = 0;
    int found = 0, limited = 0;
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < n; ++s) {
      const auto h = alg::hamiltonian_path(n, edges, s, m.order, m.prune, 1000000);
      nodes.push_back(h.nodes);
      found += h.found;
      limited += h.hit_limit;
      if (h.found) worst_solved = std::max(worst_solved, h.nodes);
    }
    const double sec = std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    std::ranges::sort(nodes);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} |\n", m.name, found, limited, (nodes[31] + nodes[32]) / 2, worst_solved, sec);
  }

  const auto tour = alg::hamiltonian_path(n, edges, 0, alg::BranchOrder::FewestOnward);
  std::vector<double> x, y;
  for (std::size_t v : tour.path) {
    x.push_back(static_cast<double>(v % k) + 0.5);
    y.push_back(static_cast<double>(k - 1 - v / k) + 0.5);
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < k; ++r)
    for (std::size_t c = 0; c < k; ++c)
      if ((r + c) % 2 == 1)
        plot.polygon({double(c), double(c + 1), double(c + 1), double(c)}, {double(r), double(r), double(r + 1), double(r + 1)},
                     dm::RGB{226, 232, 240}, true);
  plot.lines(x, y, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(x, y, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0)
      .points({x.front()}, {y.front()}, "", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 12.0)
      .points({x.back()}, {y.back()}, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 12.0);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); i += 1)
    plot.text(x[i] + 0.22, y[i] + 0.22, std::to_string(i + 1), dm::RGB{71, 85, 105}, 9.0);
  plot.legend({"start", "end"}, {dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}})
      .x_limits(0, 8)
      .y_limits(0, 8.8)
      .title("Warnsdorff knight's tour from a corner")
      .x_label("file")
      .y_label("rank")
      .size(640, 680)
      .save_svg("build/backtracking_search_tour.svg");
  std::print("\nTour from the corner: {} nodes, {} backtracks.\n", tour.nodes, tour.backtracks);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | tours found (of 64) | stopped at limit | median nodes | largest nodes (solved) | total s |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| index order | 6 | 58 | 1000000 | 787548 | 1.7 |
| index order + connectivity prune | 30 | 34 | 1000000 | 953522 | 8.6 |
| Warnsdorff order | 64 | 0 | 64 | 67771 | 0.0 |

Tour from the corner: 64 nodes, 0 backtracks.
\image(figures/backtracking_search_tour.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The knight's tour that Warnsdorff-ordered backtracking finds from the top-left corner (green) of the \(8\times8\) board; numbers give the move order and red marks the final square.)
\alttext(An 8 by 8 checkerboard with a blue path of 64 knight moves numbered 1 to 64, starting at the green top-left corner square and ending at the red square fifth from the left on the bottom row. The path first circles the edge squares and saves the centre for last.)

The order of branches matters more than anything else. In index order, backtracking found a
tour from only 6 of the 64 squares within a million nodes. The connectivity prune made each
node more expensive but cut whole subtrees, and raised that to 30. Warnsdorff's order found a
tour from all 64 squares, and from the median square it needed 64 nodes, exactly one per
square: it never backtracked. Its hardest start needed 67,771 nodes.

The figure shows why the rule works. Squares near the edge and in the corners have few knight
moves, so they are the ones that get stranded. Warnsdorff's order visits them first, sweeping
around the rim (moves 1 to 30 stay mostly in the outer two rings) and leaves the
well-connected centre for the end, when flexibility is most needed.

>> When the heuristic is not enough

Warnsdorff's rule is a heuristic, and ties (several squares with the same number of onward
moves) are broken here by square index. On larger boards that tie-break sometimes walks into a
dead end, and backtracking has to recover. The block runs Warnsdorff-ordered search from the
corner of every \(k\times k\) board for \(k=5,\dots,60\) with a limit of two million nodes, and
lists the boards where it needed to backtrack, with and without the connectivity prune.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::print("| board | Warnsdorff: nodes | backtracks | found | + prune: nodes | pruned subtrees | found |\n"
             "| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
  int clean = 0;
  for (std::size_t k = 5; k <= 60; ++k) {
    const auto edges = alg::knight_graph_edges(k, k);
    const auto a = alg::hamiltonian_path(k * k, edges, 0, alg::BranchOrder::FewestOnward, false, 2000000);
    if (a.backtracks == 0) {
      ++clean;
      continue;
    }
    const auto b = alg::hamiltonian_path(k * k, edges, 0, alg::BranchOrder::FewestOnward, true, 2000000);
    std::print("| {}×{} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", k, k, a.nodes, a.backtracks,
               a.found ? "yes" : (a.hit_limit ? "limit" : "no"), b.nodes, b.pruned,
               b.found ? "yes" : (b.hit_limit ? "limit" : "no"));
  }
  std::print("\nBoards solved with no backtracking at all: {} of 56.\n", clean);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| board | Warnsdorff: nodes | backtracks | found | + prune: nodes | pruned subtrees | found |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| 11×11 | 352 | 231 | yes | 130 | 4 | yes |
| 25×25 | 646 | 21 | yes | 634 | 4 | yes |
| 43×43 | 327776 | 325927 | yes | 1852 | 2 | yes |
| 49×49 | 2000000 | 2000000 | limit | 2413 | 8 | yes |
| 58×58 | 2000000 | 2000000 | limit | 3370 | 4 | yes |
| 60×60 | 2000000 | 2000000 | limit | 2000000 | 941133 | limit |

Boards solved with no backtracking at all: 50 of 56.
// result_end

From the corner, Warnsdorff's order completes 50 of the 56 boards without a single backtrack.
On \(11\times11\), \(25\times25\) and \(43\times43\) it makes a wrong choice but recovers, the last only
after 325,927 backtracks: a mistake early in a long path is undone only after the whole subtree
below it is exhausted. On \(49\times49\), \(58\times58\) and \(60\times60\) it does not recover
within two million nodes. The connectivity prune detects the stranded squares as soon as they
are cut off, rather than at the end of the path, and solves the 43, 49 and 58 boards in a few
thousand nodes. On \(60\times60\) it prunes 941,133 subtrees and still runs out of budget, so the
mistake must lie further back than the prune can see. Better tie-breaking rules, such as Pohl's (1967) or
Squirrel and Cull's (1996), avoid many of these failures.

> Summary

- Backtracking explores the tree of simple paths: one node per path from the start, \(O(V)\)
  memory, exponential time.
- In \(K_n\) there are exactly \(\lfloor e(n-2)!\rfloor\) simple paths between two vertices and
  \(2\lfloor e(n-2)!\rfloor\) search nodes, confirmed up to \(n=11\). Grids grow more slowly but
  still exponentially: 1,262,816 corner-to-corner paths and 18.5 million nodes on \(6\times6\).
- For Hamiltonian paths the branch order decides the outcome: on the \(8\times8\) knight's graph,
  index order solved 6 of 64 starts within a million nodes, a connectivity prune 30, and
  Warnsdorff's rule all 64, usually without backtracking.
- On larger boards the rule's ties occasionally lead it astray; backtracking with the prune
  recovers on most of them but not all.
